From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 20:38:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] netlink: do not require IFA_FLAGS, which predates Linux 3.14

This is what actually stops WiFi working on ether 20.0.

RtNetlinkAddressMessage.parse() returns null when the IFA_FLAGS attribute is
missing, on the stated assumption that it 'should always be present'. That
holds only on kernels 3.14 and newer -- the attribute was added there. msm8992
runs 3.10.108, whose include/uapi/linux/if_addr.h enum ends at IFA_MULTICAST
with no IFA_FLAGS at all.

So every RTM_NEWADDR the kernel sends is discarded as unparsable:

  E IpClient.wlan0: [NetlinkTracker/wlan0] ERROR unparsable netlink msg:
     50000000 14000000 ... 02188000 07000000 08000100 ........ ...
                                                      ^^^^^^^^ the IPv4 address

IpClient therefore never learns the interface acquired an address, so
provisioning never completes, so WifiClientModeImpl never reports CONNECTED.
ConnectivityService only calls createNativeNetwork() on CONNECTED, which is why
netd kept logging 'no such netId' and why routes and DNS could not be
installed. The network sat in CONNECTING until the 18-second timeout dropped
it, having held a perfectly valid DHCP lease the whole time.

Nothing threw and nothing crashed, which is why four rounds of chasing
exceptions -- netd, BpfNetMaps, the stats parser -- each fixed a real bug
without fixing this one. I had also seen these 'unparsable netlink msg' lines
in earlier logs and dismissed them as cosmetic noise.

The fix is to fall back to the 8 bits of flags already read from ifaddrmsg,
which is precisely what a pre-3.14 kernel reports, rather than rejecting the
message.
---
 .../util/netlink/RtNetlinkAddressMessage.java | 23 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/common/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/RtNetlinkAddressMessage.java b/common/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/RtNetlinkAddressMessage.java
index f7b0d02f..4860d0e1 100644
--- a/common/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/RtNetlinkAddressMessage.java
+++ b/common/device/com/android/net/module/util/netlink/RtNetlinkAddressMessage.java
@@ -110,14 +110,25 @@ public class RtNetlinkAddressMessage extends NetlinkMessage {
 
         // The first 8 bits of flags are in the ifaddrmsg.
         addrMsg.mFlags = addrMsg.mIfaddrmsg.flags;
-        // IFA_FLAGS. All the flags are in the IF_FLAGS attribute. This should always be present,
-        // and will overwrite the flags set above.
+        // IFA_FLAGS holds the full 32-bit flags and overwrites the 8 bits above when present.
+        //
+        // It is NOT always present: the attribute was added in Linux 3.14, and a kernel older
+        // than that simply never sends it -- msm8992 runs 3.10, whose if_addr.h enum stops at
+        // IFA_MULTICAST. Treating its absence as a parse failure discards the entire
+        // RTM_NEWADDR message, so IpClient never learns the interface acquired an address.
+        // The visible symptom is WiFi that associates, completes a DHCP lease, and then sits
+        // in CONNECTING until the 18-second timeout drops it, with nothing logged but:
+        //
+        //   E IpClient.wlan0: [NetlinkTracker/wlan0] ERROR unparsable netlink msg: ...
+        //
+        // The 8 bits from ifaddrmsg assigned above are exactly what a pre-3.14 kernel reports,
+        // so falling back to them is the correct behaviour rather than a workaround.
         byteBuffer.position(baseOffset);
         nlAttr = StructNlAttr.findNextAttrOfType(IFA_FLAGS, byteBuffer);
-        if (nlAttr == null) return null;
-        final Integer value = nlAttr.getValueAsInteger();
-        if (value == null) return null;
-        addrMsg.mFlags = value;
+        if (nlAttr != null) {
+            final Integer value = nlAttr.getValueAsInteger();
+            if (value != null) addrMsg.mFlags = value;
+        }
 
         return addrMsg;
     }
